1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:02,266
The year was 1869.

2
00:00:02,267 --> 00:00:11,080
The first transcontinental railroad had just been finished, connecting

3
00:00:11,081 --> 00:00:15,812
America's east with the distant west for the first time.

4
00:00:15,813 --> 00:00:18,600
Ahead lay awesome challenges that

5
00:00:18,601 --> 00:00:20,362
few people had imagined.

6
00:00:20,363 --> 00:00:25,500
In order for America to tame the west with the railroad, people needed

7
00:00:25,501 --> 00:00:32,580
to fix dangerous technology that killed and maimed countless railroaders, span the immense

8
00:00:32,581 --> 00:00:39,120
distances with passenger and freight trains, and quickly push new steel tracks out across

9
00:00:39,121 --> 00:00:42,300
vast unsettled territories to exploit their potential.

10
00:00:44,300 --> 00:00:50,540
It would all happen in just three decades, from the driving of the spike at Promontory

11
00:00:50,541 --> 00:00:52,720
to the turn of the century.

12
00:00:52,721 --> 00:00:57,720
This is a story of extraordinary ambition and risk-taking,

13
00:00:57,721 --> 00:01:02,720
of men who knew no limits to the potential of technology.

14
00:01:02,721 --> 00:01:07,720
Their efforts would produce the marvels of the ages,

15
00:01:07,721 --> 00:01:11,720
marvels of steel and steam and wood and speed

16
00:01:11,721 --> 00:01:15,720
that truly defined us as a nation and a world power.

17
00:01:51,500 --> 00:01:52,700
I'm sorry.

18
00:01:52,701 --> 00:01:58,480
At the start of the Civil War, Abraham Lincoln launched America's dream of connecting the

19
00:01:58,481 --> 00:02:01,140
country by rail.

20
00:02:01,141 --> 00:02:06,100
The first transcontinental railroad was the most ambitious engineering challenge of the

21
00:02:06,101 --> 00:02:08,820
19th century.

22
00:02:08,821 --> 00:02:15,200
In 1863, the two railroad companies assigned to the task the Central Pacific, building

23
00:02:15,201 --> 00:02:22,200
east from Sacramento, California, and the Union Pacific building west from Omaha, Nebraska,

24
00:02:23,140 --> 00:02:29,640
faced formidable obstacles building a 1,600-mile path through some of the most difficult terrain

25
00:02:29,641 --> 00:02:34,988
on earth, including two mountain ranges and a vast desert wasteland.

26
00:02:34,989 --> 00:02:36,640
For six brutal years,

27
00:02:39,100 --> 00:02:45,100
an army of men accomplished some of the most spectacular engineering feats of the era.

28
00:02:45,101 --> 00:02:51,840
They chipped and blasted a half-mile tunnel with homemade nitroglycerin, erected enormous

29
00:02:51,841 --> 00:03:00,160
wooden trestles, laid tracks through the great western plains under threat of Indian attack.

30
00:03:00,161 --> 00:03:04,320
Hundreds of men were killed or injured on the job.

31
00:03:04,321 --> 00:03:09,180
When these rugged railroaders closed the tracks of the first transcontinental railroad at

32
00:03:09,181 --> 00:03:16,180
Promontory, Utah on May 10, 1869, they knew they had accomplished something wonderful,

33
00:03:16,181 --> 00:03:21,151
a national work that would last through the ages.

34
00:03:21,152 --> 00:03:25,920
But the challenge to tame the West was far from

35
00:03:25,921 --> 00:03:29,639
over. In fact, it had really only just begun.

36
00:03:29,640 --> 00:03:34,020
If the railroad was to survive, the owners would have

37
00:03:34,021 --> 00:03:38,560
to find a way of expanding it from just one thin line of steel

38
00:03:38,561 --> 00:03:41,920
to thousands and thousands of miles.

39
00:03:41,921 --> 00:03:45,600
Ahead lay dangers and challenges even they could not imagine.

40
00:03:52,780 --> 00:03:55,920
Five days after the joining of the two lines,

41
00:03:55,921 --> 00:03:58,800
regular train service began.

42
00:03:58,801 --> 00:04:01,680
What had taken four to six months by wagon

43
00:04:01,681 --> 00:04:05,060
now only took seven days.

44
00:04:05,061 --> 00:04:07,960
The early journey west was a magic carpet ride

45
00:04:07,961 --> 00:04:11,600
to a mysterious and wild new world.

46
00:04:11,601 --> 00:04:14,600
The passengers experienced awesome new landscapes

47
00:04:14,601 --> 00:04:19,460
and wildlife, antelope, prairie dogs, coyotes,

48
00:04:19,461 --> 00:04:22,700
and the last remaining buffalo.

49
00:04:22,701 --> 00:04:25,040
For many travelers, seeing the Rocky Mountains

50
00:04:25,041 --> 00:04:28,100
was a childhood dream come true.

51
00:04:28,101 --> 00:04:30,640
One man wrote,

52
00:04:30,641 --> 00:04:33,700
my boyish dreams were realized.

53
00:04:33,701 --> 00:04:36,880
For hours at the school desk, I have pondered over the map

54
00:04:36,881 --> 00:04:40,960
and wandered in imagination with Lewis and Clark,

55
00:04:40,961 --> 00:04:44,160
the hunters and trappers, and early immigrants.

56
00:04:44,161 --> 00:04:48,180
And here lay the first great range in the pureness of white,

57
00:04:48,181 --> 00:04:53,160
distant to be sure, but there it lay, enshrined in beauty.

58
00:04:53,161 --> 00:04:56,380
Railroad Traveler, 1871.

59
00:04:56,381 --> 00:04:59,600
Passengers would also see a rougher, more hostile landscape

60
00:04:59,601 --> 00:05:05,120
their window, a wasteland littered with painful reminders of pioneers who had

61
00:05:05,121 --> 00:05:10,880
come before them. The early passengers quickly learned that even their own

62
00:05:10,881 --> 00:05:17,240
journey west was no smooth ride to paradise. Railroad travel was dangerous

63
00:05:17,241 --> 00:05:21,860
in the early years. Train wrecks and derailments were common, killing and

64
00:05:21,861 --> 00:05:28,500
injuring countless passengers and railroad men. Boilers blew up, bridges

65
00:05:28,501 --> 00:05:31,080
collapsed under the weight of trains.

66
00:05:31,081 --> 00:05:33,340
Brittle iron tracks cracked.

67
00:05:33,341 --> 00:05:36,600
Primitive brakes overheated and failed.

68
00:05:36,601 --> 00:05:38,620
The lack of a signal system sometimes

69
00:05:38,621 --> 00:05:40,820
caused two trains to be accidentally

70
00:05:40,821 --> 00:05:46,620
switched onto the same track and sent speeding into each other.

71
00:05:46,621 --> 00:05:48,860
The wood passenger cars seemed almost

72
00:05:48,861 --> 00:05:51,460
to self-destruct on impact.

73
00:05:51,461 --> 00:05:54,220
Passengers could be crushed and thrown out.

74
00:05:54,221 --> 00:05:56,360
Windows would shatter because there was no safety plate

75
00:05:56,361 --> 00:05:57,820
glass in that day.

76
00:05:57,821 --> 00:06:01,720
Stoves would overturn, spilling hot coals through the crushed and broken wood,

77
00:06:02,160 --> 00:06:03,920
and instantly trains were set on fire.

78
00:06:04,680 --> 00:06:06,800
Cars would fall into rivers off of bridges.

79
00:06:08,100 --> 00:06:09,760
Weather was a major problem.

80
00:06:10,520 --> 00:06:14,820
In snowstorms, the train could be stranded for days in the middle of nowhere.

81
00:06:16,600 --> 00:06:21,680
Often when we came to a stop, only the top of the stack of the front engine would be visible.

82
00:06:22,680 --> 00:06:25,740
All this time, the snow kept coming down day and night

83
00:06:25,741 --> 00:06:29,640
until the only signs of a railroad across the range

84
00:06:29,641 --> 00:06:32,220
were the tops of the telegraph poles.

85
00:06:33,260 --> 00:06:35,580
Cy Warman, 1870.

86
00:06:38,020 --> 00:06:39,540
Even in fair weather,

87
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:41,780
the first transcontinental railroad

88
00:06:41,781 --> 00:06:44,900
running from Omaha, Nebraska to Sacramento, California,

89
00:06:45,520 --> 00:06:46,220
had problems.

90
00:06:48,580 --> 00:06:50,660
In the race to build the line,

91
00:06:51,360 --> 00:06:55,400
there was naturally a long list of defects on safe bridges.

92
00:06:55,740 --> 00:07:00,180
tunnels too narrow, roadbed not properly leveled,

93
00:07:00,181 --> 00:07:04,880
poor rail alignment, and carelessly laid cross ties.

94
00:07:04,881 --> 00:07:07,880
One government inspector declared it the worst railroad

95
00:07:07,881 --> 00:07:09,720
he had ever seen.

96
00:07:09,721 --> 00:07:12,720
But that didn't stop the railroaders.

97
00:07:19,460 --> 00:07:23,440
Railroadmen risked life and limb on their job.

98
00:07:23,441 --> 00:07:26,200
Many would end up dead or permanently crippled.

99
00:07:27,400 --> 00:07:36,240
More than six times as many railroad men as passengers were killed or injured in accidents during the early days of the transcontinental railroad.

100
00:07:38,540 --> 00:07:42,100
Railroading ranked as one of America's most dangerous jobs.

101
00:07:43,400 --> 00:07:46,120
Railroads didn't so much care about the safety aspects.

102
00:07:46,560 --> 00:07:48,820
They were concerned about the bottom line and the dollar and the profit.

103
00:07:49,260 --> 00:07:50,880
So the less they had to change, the better.

104
00:07:50,881 --> 00:07:56,280
The pioneer Western Railroaders were a courageous breed.

105
00:07:57,280 --> 00:08:00,600
They endured the hardships and dangers with a sense of pride.

106
00:08:01,780 --> 00:08:07,520
In the next three decades, their territory would expand out from Missouri to the Pacific Northwest,

107
00:08:08,720 --> 00:08:11,720
from above the Canadian border to below the Mexican.

108
00:08:13,620 --> 00:08:19,980
The engineer was the hero, revered along with the mythical cowboy or gunfighter of the West.

109
00:08:19,981 --> 00:08:25,100
He was assigned to an engine for years and cared for it with pride.

110
00:08:25,800 --> 00:08:27,640
Often his life was on the line.

111
00:08:28,680 --> 00:08:35,720
Many times, if he saw disaster pending, he would instruct his firemen to bail out.

112
00:08:35,880 --> 00:08:39,840
They used to say, take to the birds, take to the birds, we're going to crash.

113
00:08:40,180 --> 00:08:43,700
And he'd stay with his locomotive, trying his best to control it.

114
00:08:44,280 --> 00:08:47,240
And many times they were killed in crashes.

115
00:08:47,241 --> 00:08:52,240
The fireman worked hard for his $2.40 a day.

116
00:08:52,241 --> 00:08:58,240
Early engines burned anywhere from 40 to 200 pounds of coal per mile.

117
00:08:58,241 --> 00:09:04,240
Sometimes a fireman might move more than two tons of coal in less than half an hour.

118
00:09:04,241 --> 00:09:10,240
One danger that was unique to Western railroading was buffalo.

119
00:09:10,241 --> 00:09:12,240
The buffalo at that time were a problem.

120
00:09:12,241 --> 00:09:16,240
They would get on the tracks preventing the train from progressing forward.

121
00:09:16,241 --> 00:09:17,480
progressing forward.

122
00:09:17,481 --> 00:09:20,240
You can imagine 1,000 buffalo on the tracks.

123
00:09:20,241 --> 00:09:23,780
On one occasion, the story goes that the buffalo had stopped

124
00:09:23,781 --> 00:09:27,120
this train, the engineer was incensed with those animals

125
00:09:27,121 --> 00:09:30,220
on the track, and so he took a shot at them.

126
00:09:30,221 --> 00:09:33,620
They stampeded and derailed the locomotive.

127
00:09:33,621 --> 00:09:36,100
One of the great ironies of the Pacific Railroad

128
00:09:36,101 --> 00:09:39,520
is that we created this wonderful 2,000 mile system

129
00:09:39,521 --> 00:09:41,380
across the continent, and yet we still

130
00:09:41,381 --> 00:09:44,960
didn't know with any real certainty how to make trains

131
00:09:44,961 --> 00:09:49,760
stop reliably and especially crossing the sierra nevada mountain range the rockies and

132
00:09:51,280 --> 00:09:57,520
the kinds of hills they faced that was really a critical factor the railroad companies quickly

133
00:09:57,521 --> 00:10:04,080
realized that if the railroad was going to expand in the west they needed to be faster and safer

134
00:10:04,960 --> 00:10:09,360
thousands of people rose to the challenge but only a few would know success

135
00:10:14,960 --> 00:10:23,360
As railroaders braved incredible dangers on the job, thousands of inventors from all walks

136
00:10:23,361 --> 00:10:28,240
of life were turning their attention to fixing the technology.

137
00:10:28,241 --> 00:10:32,380
In the mid-19th century, you could think of the railroad industry as being a combination

138
00:10:32,381 --> 00:10:37,720
of the automobile industry, the computer industry, and the television industry of our day.

139
00:10:37,721 --> 00:10:41,540
So that that's where the money was, that's where the inventors went, that's where men

140
00:10:41,541 --> 00:10:45,540
went to make their fortunes to create this industrial empire.

141
00:10:45,541 --> 00:10:53,540
The area of most intense interest was the inventions that made trains faster, more reliable,

142
00:10:53,541 --> 00:10:54,497
and safer.

143
00:10:54,498 --> 00:11:02,540
The coupler, the device that joined railroad cars together, attracted more inventors

144
00:11:02,541 --> 00:11:04,678
than any other railroad device.

145
00:11:04,679 --> 00:11:08,540
Whoever came up with the idea of how to make a safer and

146
00:11:08,541 --> 00:11:12,640
and better coupler stood to make a fortune.

147
00:11:12,641 --> 00:11:14,980
One inventor tackling the problem

148
00:11:14,981 --> 00:11:18,340
was an ex-Confederate Civil War major who worked in a dry goods

149
00:11:18,341 --> 00:11:21,380
store in Alexandria, Virginia.

150
00:11:21,381 --> 00:11:25,420
In his free time, Eli Janney took pen to paper

151
00:11:25,421 --> 00:11:28,540
searching for a solution.

152
00:11:28,541 --> 00:11:32,000
At that time, the standard method of coupling cars

153
00:11:32,001 --> 00:11:34,620
was the Lincoln pen system.

154
00:11:34,621 --> 00:11:37,880
It called for the brakeman to stand between the two cars.

155
00:11:37,881 --> 00:11:46,300
And while one of the cars was in motion, he had to hold and guide the pin into the iron hoop of the links as they came together.

156
00:11:46,820 --> 00:11:53,260
There was a split second of timing wherein you had to slip this into the pocket, hold it up, and drop the pin.

157
00:11:54,100 --> 00:11:59,000
And get your fingers extricated from that space before the car couplings literally touched.

158
00:12:00,100 --> 00:12:03,960
If that happened and your fingers weren't clear, no fingers.

159
00:12:04,640 --> 00:12:05,700
Sometimes no hands.

160
00:12:05,701 --> 00:12:13,420
if a slip and fall occurred, a man could be trapped in between the cars and gored, crushed, maimed, killed.

161
00:12:14,860 --> 00:12:19,560
The technology was so dangerous that virtually every brakeman was maimed.

162
00:12:20,640 --> 00:12:25,660
Back then, a railroad foreman could size up an applicant by looking at his hands.

163
00:12:26,720 --> 00:12:31,180
When the yardmaster or the trainmaster would ask the brakeman to see his hands,

164
00:12:31,181 --> 00:12:33,900
if he had all his digits, he was a greenhorn.

165
00:12:33,901 --> 00:12:38,820
if he had one missing he had a little experience if he had a couple missing he

166
00:12:38,821 --> 00:12:41,880
was pretty good but if he had all his fingers missing they would make a

167
00:12:41,881 --> 00:12:48,680
signalman out of him or some job that he didn't have to use his hands one day

168
00:12:48,681 --> 00:12:56,340
Eli Janney sketched out a simple design for a coupler his idea was for a kind of

169
00:12:56,341 --> 00:13:01,740
knuckle coupler that brought the cars together like two hands grasping each

170
00:13:01,741 --> 00:13:07,140
other. A lever running outside the car operated it so brakeman would no longer

171
00:13:07,141 --> 00:13:13,520
have to stand between the cars. Janie's first patent issued in April 1868 came

172
00:13:13,521 --> 00:13:20,400
to nothing but he didn't give up. Five years later he patented an improved

173
00:13:20,401 --> 00:13:26,980
design that was sheer genius. A crucial advantage of Janie's new coupler was its

174
00:13:26,981 --> 00:13:34,660
ability to allow cars to easily couple even when they were not at the exact same levels.

175
00:13:34,661 --> 00:13:36,880
But he didn't stop there.

176
00:13:36,881 --> 00:13:41,940
The practice at that time was for inventors to sell their new technology directly to railroad

177
00:13:41,941 --> 00:13:43,760
companies.

178
00:13:43,761 --> 00:13:48,400
They had to take their invention and actually go from railroad office to railroad office

179
00:13:48,401 --> 00:13:54,300
begging, pleading, advertising and promoting.

180
00:13:54,301 --> 00:14:00,920
The railroads were very conservative, and they were constantly looking for better inventions,

181
00:14:00,921 --> 00:14:06,020
better equipment, but they're also very careful about many of the harebrained schemes.

182
00:14:06,021 --> 00:14:11,640
What Janney was able to do, though, is demonstrate a workable, relatively inexpensive piece of

183
00:14:11,641 --> 00:14:17,680
metal that dramatically improved the safety of passenger trains at first.

184
00:14:17,681 --> 00:14:23,340
At the same time Janney was perfecting his automatic coupler, another inventor was determined

185
00:14:23,341 --> 00:14:27,780
to overhaul the railroad's braking system.

186
00:14:27,781 --> 00:14:30,120
George Westinghouse grew up literally

187
00:14:30,121 --> 00:14:33,760
in his father's machine shop in Schenectady, New York,

188
00:14:33,761 --> 00:14:36,960
working with tools and machines and men

189
00:14:36,961 --> 00:14:39,040
who knew how to make things.

190
00:14:39,041 --> 00:14:41,220
When he was a young man, he wanted to be a leader.

191
00:14:41,221 --> 00:14:44,660
He wanted to take that boundless energy that so characterized

192
00:14:44,661 --> 00:14:48,100
the inventors of the day and use it to make his fortune,

193
00:14:48,101 --> 00:14:51,980
to make an empire, but make it out of iron and wire and parts.

194
00:14:53,340 --> 00:14:58,220
Back then, the braking on trains was not done by the engineer in the cab, but by hand,

195
00:14:58,221 --> 00:15:02,580
in a dangerous maneuver where one slip could cost the brakeman his life.

196
00:15:03,420 --> 00:15:06,780
We had to ride out on top when the train was moving.

197
00:15:07,380 --> 00:15:13,840
That took nerve, coordination, timing, and a perfect sense of balance to go over the top of a freight train.

198
00:15:14,780 --> 00:15:22,840
Winter or summer, rain, snow, sleet, ice all over the roofs, and on brake wheels and handholds.

199
00:15:22,841 --> 00:15:25,740
Dick Nelson, 1871.

200
00:15:27,120 --> 00:15:30,680
Here was one more way that brakemen could lose their lives.

201
00:15:31,580 --> 00:15:33,960
Not only were the handbrakes hard to reach,

202
00:15:34,420 --> 00:15:35,980
they were difficult to operate.

203
00:15:37,120 --> 00:15:39,460
Uneven braking could cause a train to separate.

204
00:15:40,600 --> 00:15:42,400
If brakes were applied too tightly,

205
00:15:43,060 --> 00:15:44,300
friction heated the wheels,

206
00:15:44,860 --> 00:15:47,500
causing them to slide dangerously along the track.

207
00:15:48,940 --> 00:15:51,140
Brakemen would be forced to make quick adjustments

208
00:15:51,141 --> 00:15:54,860
on several cars to keep the train from speeding out of control.

209
00:15:56,980 --> 00:16:02,100
George Westinghouse explored ways of improving the braking system by using compressed air.

210
00:16:03,660 --> 00:16:10,400
The idea of using air to somehow stop trains and control their speed had been around for decades.

211
00:16:11,460 --> 00:16:15,120
There had been steam brakes, there had been very crude air brakes before that,

212
00:16:15,440 --> 00:16:18,660
but they were neither reliable nor did they really work together as a system.

213
00:16:18,661 --> 00:16:26,520
Westinghouse became aware of the use of compressed air to run drills in tunnels in Europe,

214
00:16:26,680 --> 00:16:32,640
long railroad tunnels, and thought that if they could run air through a rubber hose 5,000 feet

215
00:16:32,641 --> 00:16:36,840
in Europe to run a drill, well, by God, he could run air through a hose underneath a train

216
00:16:36,841 --> 00:16:40,360
and use it to stop the rolling motion.

217
00:16:42,800 --> 00:16:47,440
Westinghouse's breakthrough for an automatic brake was that each car would carry its own

218
00:16:47,441 --> 00:16:53,240
reservoir of compressed air. The engineer with a valve in the cab could regulate the

219
00:16:53,241 --> 00:16:58,380
air pressure in a train line, which was a continuous pipe running the length of the

220
00:16:58,381 --> 00:17:02,940
train connecting the air brake control valves on each car.

221
00:17:02,941 --> 00:17:05,220
By regulating the pressure in

222
00:17:05,221 --> 00:17:13,220
that train line, the engineer could apply and release all the brakes on that train simultaneously.

223
00:17:13,221 --> 00:17:20,860
Westinghouse built his first automatic air brake factory in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania in 1875.

224
00:17:20,861 --> 00:17:28,260
Both Janney's automatic coupler and Westinghouse's air brake promised a far safer future for railroading.

225
00:17:28,261 --> 00:17:35,080
But in a cold financial calculation, both inventions were slow to be adopted by the railroad industry.

226
00:17:35,081 --> 00:17:38,000
It came down to dollars and cents.

227
00:17:38,001 --> 00:17:41,000
The railroad could hire a brakeman for $1.50 a day.

228
00:17:41,001 --> 00:17:42,740
That brakeman got hurt or killed.

229
00:17:43,460 --> 00:17:46,580
Just about their only responsibility was to move the body off the tracks

230
00:17:46,581 --> 00:17:47,920
and send it home in a basket.

231
00:17:48,560 --> 00:17:53,820
It was cheaper on the whole for the railroads just to continue maiming and killing its employees

232
00:17:53,821 --> 00:17:59,360
than it was to actually invest millions of dollars in these new technologies.

233
00:18:00,640 --> 00:18:06,340
Perhaps the strongest motivation for the railroad companies to install air brakes and automatic couplers

234
00:18:06,341 --> 00:18:08,260
was the public outcry.

235
00:18:08,261 --> 00:18:13,000
People were growing more outraged by the carnage on the rails.

236
00:18:13,001 --> 00:18:19,920
At last, in 1893, Congress passed the Railroad Safety Appliance Act

237
00:18:19,921 --> 00:18:26,920
that required the railroads to apply the air brakes, automatic couplers, and other safety features.

238
00:18:28,100 --> 00:18:34,060
It was a watershed piece of legislation and the beginning of the process of regulating industrial safety.

239
00:18:34,061 --> 00:18:44,720
In an ironic twist in history, this safety technology that the railroads feared would cost too much actually improved productivity and saved money.

240
00:18:45,540 --> 00:18:52,560
The automatic coupler made it possible to hitch up cars faster and without the need for human intervention in each coupling.

241
00:18:53,500 --> 00:18:59,500
While the air brakes permitted the railroads to operate longer and heavier trains far more reliably.

242
00:18:59,501 --> 00:19:06,500
While Janie and Westinghouse were worrying about safety, there was another man who dreamed

243
00:19:06,501 --> 00:19:09,500
of something far grander, luxury.

244
00:19:16,500 --> 00:19:22,500
In the 1870s, affluent East Coast types rented private railroad cars for hunting expeditions

245
00:19:22,501 --> 00:19:24,500
in the Wild West.

246
00:19:24,501 --> 00:19:29,500
This luxury would cost $300 per week.

247
00:19:29,501 --> 00:19:36,500
Jerome Marble, a Massachusetts manufacturer and sportsman, hired one of the earliest hunting

248
00:19:36,501 --> 00:19:40,500
cars for his family to camp on the Dakota Plains.

249
00:19:40,501 --> 00:19:46,500
The car came fully loaded with gun racks, dog kennels, and large ice boxes to preserve

250
00:19:46,501 --> 00:19:48,500
trophies of the hunt.

251
00:19:48,501 --> 00:19:54,500
The Mobile Hunting Lodge was just one of the more extravagant innovations of a man who was

252
00:19:54,501 --> 00:19:58,100
to make his fortune on railroad passenger travel,

253
00:19:58,101 --> 00:20:01,000
George Mortimer Pullman.

254
00:20:01,001 --> 00:20:04,440
Like so many other great late 19th century men,

255
00:20:04,441 --> 00:20:07,440
George Pullman was from the Northeast,

256
00:20:07,441 --> 00:20:12,460
born in Brockton, New York on March 3rd, 1831.

257
00:20:12,461 --> 00:20:17,960
As a young man, he headed to Chicago to seek his fortune.

258
00:20:17,961 --> 00:20:19,960
Even before ground was broken

259
00:20:19,961 --> 00:20:22,400
for the first transcontinental railroad,

260
00:20:22,401 --> 00:20:28,740
Pullman realized that long-distance travelers would need comfort to make the journey tolerable.

261
00:20:29,940 --> 00:20:33,600
On one of his trips west from New York State, on his way to Chicago,

262
00:20:34,120 --> 00:20:37,480
George Pullman rented a sleeping car berth for the dollar it cost

263
00:20:37,481 --> 00:20:40,220
and climbed into it just to see how bad it was.

264
00:20:40,320 --> 00:20:43,840
And it was dark and cramped and small, and he thought it was atrocious.

265
00:20:44,680 --> 00:20:49,260
And legend has it that at that point he decided that was a way he could make his fortune.

266
00:20:49,261 --> 00:20:57,560
His lifetime ambition was born to replicate the elegance and comfort of first-class hotels on wheels.

267
00:20:59,360 --> 00:21:06,380
Other innovators experimented with sleepers, but Pullman was the one who came up with the idea of hinged upper berths

268
00:21:06,381 --> 00:21:09,340
that could be folded against the ceiling during the day.

269
00:21:10,220 --> 00:21:13,380
And the reclining seat could double as a bed.

270
00:21:13,381 --> 00:21:25,340
In 1864, he invested his entire savings of $20,000 to build the magnificent sleeper car he called the Pioneer.

271
00:21:26,560 --> 00:21:28,920
No expense was spared in the craftsmanship.

272
00:21:29,620 --> 00:21:34,520
Each Pullman sleeping car mirrored the ornate Victorian style that was sweeping America.

273
00:21:34,521 --> 00:21:41,460
By the time transcontinental travel became popular, George Pullman had the market for

274
00:21:41,461 --> 00:21:43,520
overnight luxury railroading sewn up.

275
00:21:44,740 --> 00:21:51,580
In the 1870s, he was leasing to the Western Lines his own fleet of cars, fully equipped

276
00:21:51,581 --> 00:21:52,560
and staffed.

277
00:21:53,420 --> 00:21:59,500
In 1870, the price of a first-class ticket between Omaha and Sacramento was $100, equivalent

278
00:21:59,501 --> 00:22:01,460
to over $3,000 today.

279
00:22:01,461 --> 00:22:08,400
Providing comfort was not the only problem that Pullman decided to tackle.

280
00:22:08,401 --> 00:22:10,440
People needed to eat.

281
00:22:10,441 --> 00:22:14,680
In the early years of transcontinental rail travel, passengers ate their meals during

282
00:22:14,681 --> 00:22:19,260
short stops at stations along the way.

283
00:22:19,261 --> 00:22:23,020
We found the quality of the food and the whole bad.

284
00:22:23,021 --> 00:22:25,220
All three meals were almost identical.

285
00:22:25,221 --> 00:22:31,220
Tea, buffalo steaks, antelope chops, sweet potatoes and boiled Indian corn with whole

286
00:22:31,221 --> 00:22:37,035
cakes and syrup ad nauseam. William Robertson, 1873.

287
00:22:37,036 --> 00:22:41,060
One traveler having dined happily on

288
00:22:41,061 --> 00:22:46,960
a delicious chicken stew for breakfast was later upset to learn that he had eaten prairie

289
00:22:46,961 --> 00:22:47,661
dog.

290
00:22:47,662 --> 00:22:52,080
Pullman guessed that this was no way to attract the rich clientele he was aiming

291
00:22:52,081 --> 00:22:52,781
for.

292
00:22:52,782 --> 00:23:00,340
In 1868 he introduced the most elegant dining car of its day, named the Delmonico

293
00:23:00,341 --> 00:23:03,340
after a famous restaurant owner in New York City.

294
00:23:05,680 --> 00:23:09,240
The Pullman staff turned out 12 course meals.

295
00:23:09,241 --> 00:23:12,180
Women were proud to have gained five to 10 pounds

296
00:23:12,181 --> 00:23:16,900
on a one month round trip journey aboard a Hotel Express.

297
00:23:16,901 --> 00:23:21,020
The foods were the best of ducks and fowl,

298
00:23:21,021 --> 00:23:25,000
roast beef, roast ham, boiled ham,

299
00:23:25,001 --> 00:23:27,840
fresh potatoes, fresh baked breads,

300
00:23:27,841 --> 00:23:32,020
Desserts made on board, even fresh Rocky Mountain trout,

301
00:23:32,520 --> 00:23:34,380
when crossing over the Continental Divide,

302
00:23:34,520 --> 00:23:37,100
caught and delivered directly to the train.

303
00:23:37,740 --> 00:23:39,360
Most of the cars even carried ice

304
00:23:39,361 --> 00:23:42,580
and could serve ice cream and exotic frozen desserts,

305
00:23:43,240 --> 00:23:46,100
which for people traveling across the desert in the late 19th century

306
00:23:46,101 --> 00:23:47,500
was nothing short of miraculous.

307
00:23:48,340 --> 00:23:50,360
With first-class business booming,

308
00:23:50,980 --> 00:23:53,720
Pullman built cars for smoking, reading,

309
00:23:54,360 --> 00:23:56,540
listening to music, and lounging.

310
00:23:57,840 --> 00:24:01,340
The decor included etched and stained plate glass,

311
00:24:01,341 --> 00:24:05,340
plush seat cushions, marble and walnut wash stands,

312
00:24:05,341 --> 00:24:10,340
damask curtains, and massive mirrors and frames of gilded walnut.

313
00:24:12,340 --> 00:24:16,840
The Pullman cars were a symbol of America's manufacturing supremacy,

314
00:24:16,841 --> 00:24:19,340
offering unrivaled elegance.

315
00:24:19,341 --> 00:24:23,340
But not all travelers were as enchanted with their journeys.

316
00:24:27,840 --> 00:24:36,020
The people with the roughest rides were the millions of third-class passengers who rode

317
00:24:36,021 --> 00:24:41,920
in cramped and stifling coaches known as immigrant cars.

318
00:24:41,921 --> 00:24:46,520
These trains earned their name for the role they played in America.

319
00:24:46,521 --> 00:24:51,340
Throughout the 19th and early 20th century, the railroad took the millions of immigrants

320
00:24:51,341 --> 00:24:56,900
pouring into the East Coast ports and distributed them out across the continent.

321
00:24:56,901 --> 00:25:01,400
There was no other way we could have settled this land without the immigrants

322
00:25:01,401 --> 00:25:05,540
or without the railroads. Their problem though was that they needed to go

323
00:25:05,541 --> 00:25:10,460
cheaply. They didn't care about time, they weren't concerned with comfort, but they

324
00:25:10,461 --> 00:25:15,900
needed to get where they were going for a penny a mile or even less. As a young

325
00:25:15,901 --> 00:25:20,100
man the Scottish writer Robert Louis Stevenson rode west in an immigrant

326
00:25:20,101 --> 00:25:28,720
train in contrast to the elegance of Pullman cars Stevenson's compartment was overcrowded

327
00:25:28,721 --> 00:25:37,340
unsanitary and positively bone rattling more fit for transporting cattle than people travelers

328
00:25:37,341 --> 00:25:45,300
set on unpadded wooden benches for days on end he wrote how the car was a long narrow wooden box

329
00:25:45,301 --> 00:25:51,080
like a flat roof Noah's Ark with a stove at one end and a toilet on the other.

330
00:25:51,081 --> 00:25:54,680
The benches are too short for anything but a young child.

331
00:25:54,681 --> 00:26:00,540
With a scarce elbow room for two to sit, there would not be space enough for one to lie.

332
00:26:00,541 --> 00:26:02,480
Robert Louis Stevenson.

333
00:26:02,481 --> 00:26:08,120
The aspiring writer arrived in California after ten bone-jarring days.

334
00:26:08,121 --> 00:26:11,860
Sick as a dog, but immensely relieved.

335
00:26:11,861 --> 00:26:14,860
What enticed people to take the train west?

336
00:26:14,861 --> 00:26:18,860
The answer was a combination of opportunities and freedom,

337
00:26:18,861 --> 00:26:22,860
and the railroad company's shrewd promotional campaign.

338
00:26:22,861 --> 00:26:27,860
For the railroad to fully exploit its potential in the west,

339
00:26:27,861 --> 00:26:31,860
the railroad bosses needed to attract not just thousands,

340
00:26:31,861 --> 00:26:34,860
but millions of settlers.

341
00:26:41,860 --> 00:26:46,860
In the two decades following the opening of the first Pacific line,

342
00:26:46,861 --> 00:26:52,860
dozens of other railroad companies stepped up to the plate to fully exploit the West.

343
00:26:52,861 --> 00:26:58,860
Everyone wanted to own a railroad that ended with the name Pacific.

344
00:26:58,861 --> 00:27:04,860
At stake were vast fortunes to be made from freight and passenger traffic.

345
00:27:04,861 --> 00:27:10,860
Four major transcontinental railroads, the Acheson, Topeka, and Santa Fe,

346
00:27:10,861 --> 00:27:19,580
the Southern Pacific, the Northern Pacific, and the Great Northern Railway would all chart

347
00:27:19,581 --> 00:27:23,260
new paths westward.

348
00:27:23,261 --> 00:27:28,180
Thousands of miles of shorter lines would be built to exploit the West's potential,

349
00:27:28,181 --> 00:27:34,580
the vast tracts of fertile soil, millions of acres of virgin forests, mineral deposits

350
00:27:34,581 --> 00:27:38,560
such as gold, silver, and copper.

351
00:27:38,561 --> 00:27:43,320
To encourage building, the government gave railroad companies land grants totaling over

352
00:27:43,321 --> 00:27:50,780
155 million acres with the right to sell it at a profit.

353
00:27:50,781 --> 00:27:56,460
In the mid and late 19th century, railroad executives engaged in an all-out battle with

354
00:27:56,461 --> 00:28:01,080
their competitors to expand their territory.

355
00:28:01,081 --> 00:28:06,640
Fighting on the front line of the battles were the survey teams, each one out there

356
00:28:06,641 --> 00:28:10,480
for their own railroad, trying to stake out their own route,

357
00:28:10,481 --> 00:28:14,300
the best route, sometimes racing to stake a claim

358
00:28:14,301 --> 00:28:16,880
on the same mountain pass.

359
00:28:16,881 --> 00:28:20,600
In Colorado, in New Mexico, throughout the Rockies,

360
00:28:20,601 --> 00:28:22,760
these survey teams would often tangle with each other.

361
00:28:22,761 --> 00:28:24,180
There was gunfire.

362
00:28:24,181 --> 00:28:26,840
There was intrigue in the night, men staking out

363
00:28:26,841 --> 00:28:29,140
routes by torchlight and bonfires,

364
00:28:29,141 --> 00:28:31,620
trying to get there before the other guy.

365
00:28:31,621 --> 00:28:34,920
And in that still relatively lawless area,

366
00:28:34,921 --> 00:28:36,160
Just about anything went.

367
00:28:36,560 --> 00:28:38,400
The person who got there first with the most

368
00:28:38,401 --> 00:28:41,900
was the one who got the best route and probably won the game.

369
00:28:42,840 --> 00:28:46,520
But winning the game depended on more than staking out the best route.

370
00:28:47,440 --> 00:28:50,200
The railroads needed settlers more than anything else.

371
00:28:50,780 --> 00:28:52,200
The settlers were their customers.

372
00:28:52,800 --> 00:28:53,580
They were the passengers.

373
00:28:53,760 --> 00:28:55,100
They were the ones who shipped the wheat,

374
00:28:55,520 --> 00:28:57,920
who imported the machinery, who needed the fertilizer.

375
00:28:58,540 --> 00:29:02,260
If it wasn't for the settlers, not thousands but millions of them,

376
00:29:02,880 --> 00:29:04,780
the railroads would have had no reason for being.

377
00:29:04,920 --> 00:29:11,920
The government paved the way for settlers with the Federal Homestead Act of 1862.

378
00:29:11,921 --> 00:29:19,920
American citizens and permanent immigrants could purchase 160 acres of public land for only $10

379
00:29:19,921 --> 00:29:25,920
after five years of residence. In order to sell as much land as possible,

380
00:29:25,921 --> 00:29:31,920
railroad companies began competing for the immigrants. Railroad-sponsored posters,

381
00:29:31,921 --> 00:29:38,400
posters, pamphlets, and books promised religious and political freedom, cheap, fertile land,

382
00:29:38,401 --> 00:29:41,082
and low-priced railroad tickets.

383
00:29:41,083 --> 00:29:45,440
The truth was often stretched or completely ignored.

384
00:29:47,280 --> 00:29:53,360
California, the cornucopia of the world, room for millions of immigrants in a climate for health

385
00:29:53,361 --> 00:29:54,083
and wealth.

386
00:29:54,084 --> 00:29:59,340
At the same time, the railroad sent hundreds of railroad pitchmen throughout the

387
00:29:59,341 --> 00:30:08,840
East Coast, Europe, and parts of Asia to recruit as many land-hungry settlers as possible.

388
00:30:08,841 --> 00:30:15,520
In the late 19th century, millions of men, women, and children from mainly Germany, Ireland,

389
00:30:15,521 --> 00:30:23,960
Italy, Scotland, Scandinavia, and Russia journeyed west by rail, bringing with them the dream

390
00:30:23,961 --> 00:30:28,960
of freedom and opportunity and the hope for a better life.

391
00:30:30,660 --> 00:30:32,960
Of the 16 and a half million immigrants

392
00:30:32,961 --> 00:30:35,500
who came to America during the second half

393
00:30:35,501 --> 00:30:39,340
of the 19th century, almost 11 million immigrants

394
00:30:39,341 --> 00:30:42,140
boarded trains to find their new homes.

395
00:30:43,920 --> 00:30:46,120
Joining the human wave were the legions

396
00:30:46,121 --> 00:30:50,160
of American citizens who wanted to relocate to the West.

397
00:30:50,161 --> 00:30:54,160
The population in the West skyrocketed.

398
00:30:54,161 --> 00:31:00,160
Railroad technology brought some of the fastest, most profound changes in human history.

399
00:31:00,161 --> 00:31:06,160
The Western railroads did not link towns, they created them.

400
00:31:06,161 --> 00:31:10,160
People settled around where the railroad stations were built.

401
00:31:10,161 --> 00:31:14,160
And much of this pattern is still visible today.

402
00:31:14,161 --> 00:31:21,660
The town of Denver, Colorado grew because the railroads chose it as a junction point at the base of the Rockies.

403
00:31:22,860 --> 00:31:29,400
But hundreds of other western towns declined or died if the railroad bosses built their lines elsewhere.

404
00:31:31,000 --> 00:31:36,500
The settlement the west brought on by the railroad radically transformed the land.

405
00:31:37,460 --> 00:31:41,100
Prairies were dug up to create farms for corn and wheat.

406
00:31:42,040 --> 00:31:44,980
Virgin forests were cut for new housing construction

407
00:31:44,981 --> 00:31:47,760
and lumber to build and run the railroad.

408
00:31:48,660 --> 00:31:51,440
All of this growth generated the real moneymaker

409
00:31:51,441 --> 00:31:54,200
for the Western Railroads, freight transportation.

410
00:32:01,220 --> 00:32:04,560
The expansion of the Western Railroad's freight traffic

411
00:32:04,561 --> 00:32:07,760
revolutionized the way America did business.

412
00:32:07,761 --> 00:32:14,900
For the first time, wheat grown on farms in the Midwest could be shipped to the East Coast at a profit.

413
00:32:16,260 --> 00:32:21,200
Iron ore from Minnesota could be quickly shipped to the blast furnaces of Pittsburgh.

414
00:32:22,720 --> 00:32:25,580
When the first transcontinental railroad opened,

415
00:32:26,280 --> 00:32:31,340
freight was transported by boxcars and flat cars that hauled everything under the sun.

416
00:32:33,100 --> 00:32:35,940
Within a decade, refrigerator cars were invented.

417
00:32:35,941 --> 00:32:43,320
They were essentially boxcars insulated with cork and cooled with ice.

418
00:32:43,321 --> 00:32:48,540
Refrigerated cars allowed perishable goods to be transported across long distances without

419
00:32:48,541 --> 00:32:50,620
spoilage.

420
00:32:50,621 --> 00:32:55,940
One of the first refrigerated cargo left Baltimore, Maryland for California with a shipment of

421
00:32:55,941 --> 00:32:59,600
fresh Chesapeake Bay oysters.

422
00:32:59,601 --> 00:33:05,280
that insulated boxcar they placed these four large hog head barrels of oysters

423
00:33:05,281 --> 00:33:11,540
around the oysters they packed ice and they had stops along the route where

424
00:33:11,541 --> 00:33:16,640
they could pick up more ice and pack it tighter with ice as the ice melted but

425
00:33:16,641 --> 00:33:22,820
that refrigerated cargo got to San Francisco within a week and they say

426
00:33:22,821 --> 00:33:27,680
that the men were waiting for that and they actually were almost auctioning off

427
00:33:27,681 --> 00:33:28,765
those oysters.

428
00:33:28,766 --> 00:33:35,120
The advent of the refrigerator car allowed people to obtain fresh food further and

429
00:33:35,121 --> 00:33:37,325
further away from its source.

430
00:33:37,326 --> 00:33:42,800
Fresh vegetables from California or the far west could be transported to

431
00:33:42,801 --> 00:33:48,538
every part of the country at all times of the year, including the dead of winter.

432
00:33:48,539 --> 00:33:49,600
People's eating

433
00:33:49,601 --> 00:33:52,160
habits would be changed forever.

434
00:33:52,161 --> 00:33:57,360
The refrigerator car created markets worth fortunes. It gave rise

435
00:33:57,361 --> 00:34:00,160
to the meat packing industry of Chicago,

436
00:34:00,161 --> 00:34:02,460
allowing for dressed and packed meats

437
00:34:02,461 --> 00:34:05,300
to now be transported to local stores

438
00:34:05,301 --> 00:34:07,540
throughout the country.

439
00:34:07,541 --> 00:34:09,880
This vast transportation network depended

440
00:34:09,881 --> 00:34:12,240
on maximum speed and efficiency

441
00:34:12,241 --> 00:34:15,640
and required something we take for granted today,

442
00:34:15,641 --> 00:34:17,640
standard time.

443
00:34:24,220 --> 00:34:26,620
In the 19th century, there was no uniform

444
00:34:26,621 --> 00:34:32,140
national time system. Most people live their lives according to the sun and by

445
00:34:32,141 --> 00:34:38,320
the ebb and flow of the seasons. Noon was when the sun passed directly overhead

446
00:34:38,321 --> 00:34:45,180
which resulted in a complex patchwork of time zones. When the railroads permitted

447
00:34:45,181 --> 00:34:51,460
rapid travel over the continent, the need for larger unified time zones became

448
00:34:51,461 --> 00:34:57,240
critical. Railroads needed to have precise scheduling where lines crossed

449
00:34:57,241 --> 00:35:03,760
and at stations where trains departed and left. Not having a standardized time

450
00:35:03,761 --> 00:35:08,120
system could actually cause railroad accidents and just simply made

451
00:35:08,121 --> 00:35:16,000
operations inefficient. In 1883 the major American railroads held a convention and

452
00:35:16,001 --> 00:35:20,100
and adapted what they called Railroad Standard Time,

453
00:35:20,800 --> 00:35:24,000
which divided the United States into four time zones.

454
00:35:25,180 --> 00:35:28,100
On an appointed day, all railroads in America

455
00:35:28,101 --> 00:35:31,940
switched their clocks to follow these new standard time zones.

456
00:35:32,960 --> 00:35:37,700
This completely revolutionized America's concept of time.

457
00:35:38,920 --> 00:35:43,400
In addition to train schedules, the railroads required extraordinary capital,

458
00:35:44,040 --> 00:35:48,020
More money than any other technology in the 19th century.

459
00:35:48,640 --> 00:35:50,860
The stakes were incredibly high.

460
00:35:51,420 --> 00:35:55,840
The landscape of American business history is littered with the carcasses of railroads

461
00:35:55,841 --> 00:35:59,260
that seemed like they were a good idea at the time and failed,

462
00:35:59,960 --> 00:36:05,460
or schemes that promoters put together merely to skin the public and reap the profits and run.

463
00:36:06,060 --> 00:36:09,360
Ultimately, they began falling together into bigger and bigger systems

464
00:36:09,361 --> 00:36:15,660
as these companies consolidated into actually workable networks in the late 19th century.

465
00:36:16,740 --> 00:36:20,400
One of the biggest networks to emerge began with an ambition

466
00:36:20,401 --> 00:36:25,040
that would stop at nothing short of exploiting America's last untamed wilderness.

467
00:36:33,400 --> 00:36:38,860
One man knew the scent of money and a frontier in America that had not been exploited.

468
00:36:39,360 --> 00:36:42,760
the great northern expanse leading to the Pacific.

469
00:36:44,000 --> 00:36:47,640
James Jerome Hill set out to build what would become

470
00:36:47,641 --> 00:36:50,500
the last railroad empire of the 19th century,

471
00:36:51,380 --> 00:36:53,300
the Great Northern Railway.

472
00:36:54,500 --> 00:36:57,880
As a child growing up on a farm in Ontario, Canada,

473
00:36:58,420 --> 00:37:01,120
Jim Hill had lost an eye to a playmate's arrow,

474
00:37:01,440 --> 00:37:05,520
but few men looking westward had as clear a vision.

475
00:37:05,521 --> 00:37:13,680
his battles with rivals won him plenty of nicknames little giant empire builder

476
00:37:13,681 --> 00:37:23,040
oregon bandit red river pirate and one-eyed old son of a bitch people who knew him remarked that

477
00:37:23,041 --> 00:37:30,000
he had a mind like a steel trap he absorbed information he loved facts but more than that

478
00:37:30,001 --> 00:37:35,240
he always had a vision he set out to be a sailor as a young man didn't get farther than the port

479
00:37:35,241 --> 00:37:37,661
of New York and then headed west, made it to St.

480
00:37:37,662 --> 00:37:39,780
Paul, which was then a tiny little village

481
00:37:39,781 --> 00:37:44,560
at the head of the Mississippi River navigation, and remained there the rest of his life.

482
00:37:45,700 --> 00:37:52,180
In 1878, Jim Hill and three other men acquired the dilapidated St. Paul in Pacific

483
00:37:52,181 --> 00:37:55,612
and set their sights on the Pacific Northwest.

484
00:37:55,613 --> 00:37:58,820
They call their railroad the Great Northern

485
00:37:58,821 --> 00:38:02,280
and pushed west from St. Paul towards Seattle,

486
00:38:03,180 --> 00:38:06,160
building or acquiring track along the way.

487
00:38:07,000 --> 00:38:10,360
Jim Hill was the ringleader and the guiding force behind it,

488
00:38:10,800 --> 00:38:13,500
but these four men built it largely with their own capital

489
00:38:13,501 --> 00:38:16,460
and money they were able to raise from private investors in the East.

490
00:38:17,020 --> 00:38:21,760
Their philosophy was to build well, build permanently, and build a lot of track.

491
00:38:22,940 --> 00:38:26,880
Unlike other railroad bosses of the day who sat in their New York offices,

492
00:38:26,881 --> 00:38:30,880
He'll personally manage the project.

493
00:38:30,881 --> 00:38:35,880
Under his command, the Great Northern Railway made incredible progress.

494
00:38:35,881 --> 00:38:41,880
In one season alone, 1887, his army of 10,000 graders and bridge builders,

495
00:38:41,881 --> 00:38:51,880
along with 3,300 teams of horses, built across 640 miles of the North Dakota and Montana wilderness.

496
00:38:51,881 --> 00:38:56,640
The biggest obstacle was finding a pass through the Rocky Mountains.

497
00:38:56,641 --> 00:39:00,840
Hill was convinced there had to be a right pass there.

498
00:39:00,841 --> 00:39:05,040
To tackle the problem, Hill sent his brilliant young chief engineer,

499
00:39:05,041 --> 00:39:10,920
John F. Stevens. Stevens reached his goal on December the 11th,

500
00:39:10,921 --> 00:39:13,840
1889, despite almost freezing to death.

501
00:39:13,841 --> 00:39:17,220
Marius Pass,

502
00:39:17,221 --> 00:39:21,840
at an elevation of 5,215 feet, turned out to be the lowest

503
00:39:21,841 --> 00:39:28,169
point on the Continental Divide from Canada South almost to Mexico.

504
00:39:28,170 --> 00:39:31,380
Along his steel path, Hill created

505
00:39:31,381 --> 00:39:38,123
a sophisticated artery of feeder lines resembling tributaries of a river.

506
00:39:38,124 --> 00:39:40,340
Every few miles a branch

507
00:39:40,341 --> 00:39:46,828
line left the Great Northern's main track to tap into a potential source of revenue.

508
00:39:46,829 --> 00:39:47,600
In time he

509
00:39:47,601 --> 00:39:54,400
reach the Iron Range of Minnesota, the copper deposits of Butte, Montana, the

510
00:39:54,401 --> 00:39:59,600
forests of Washington. Thousands of great northern freight trains would haul these

511
00:39:59,601 --> 00:40:05,600
products toward the eastern markets. A cartoon poked fun of Hill's ambition.

512
00:40:05,601 --> 00:40:11,060
What it didn't capture was his drive for filling the land with people and jobs,

513
00:40:11,061 --> 00:40:15,060
the key to a railroad success.

514
00:40:15,061 --> 00:40:21,060
He persuaded one friend in Boston to start an apple orchard in Washington.

515
00:40:21,061 --> 00:40:26,060
That ranch would eventually need four and a half miles of great northern boxcars

516
00:40:26,061 --> 00:40:30,060
to ship its annual crop.

517
00:40:30,061 --> 00:40:34,060
Jim Hill encouraged a lumber baron from Minnesota to launch what would become

518
00:40:34,061 --> 00:40:38,060
the vast timber industry in the northwest.

519
00:40:38,061 --> 00:40:43,060
Some of the most massive trees on the continent were chopped down.

520
00:40:43,061 --> 00:40:49,060
Great swatches of pristine forests were cut to clear right of ways for the railroad.

521
00:40:49,061 --> 00:40:57,060
On January 6, 1893, Jim Hill's Great Northern Railway was officially finished.

522
00:40:57,061 --> 00:41:05,060
After less than 15 years, Hill had built a railroad empire, 25,000 miles of tracks.

523
00:41:05,061 --> 00:41:12,060
Hillsoon controlled 33.3% of all the railroad business in the West.

524
00:41:12,061 --> 00:41:15,060
With five transcontinental railroads now completed,

525
00:41:15,061 --> 00:41:22,060
the ceremonial striking of the Great Northern's last spike was a mere footnote in history.

526
00:41:22,061 --> 00:41:27,060
It was a bitterly cold and snowy afternoon in the Cascade Mountains.

527
00:41:27,061 --> 00:41:31,060
The workmen only reluctantly left the warmth of their cook shack

528
00:41:31,061 --> 00:41:35,060
to put on a show of celebration for the photographer.

529
00:41:35,061 --> 00:41:39,560
As for the empire builder who did most to bring it about,

530
00:41:39,561 --> 00:41:43,300
he was bedridden with rheumatism in the master bedroom

531
00:41:43,301 --> 00:41:46,900
of his new mansion in St. Paul.

532
00:41:46,901 --> 00:41:50,180
The completion of Hills Railroads symbolized the end

533
00:41:50,181 --> 00:41:53,940
of America's most ambitious building spree.

534
00:41:53,941 --> 00:41:57,260
But the impact of the Western Railroads lived on,

535
00:41:57,261 --> 00:42:01,060
ushering in the modern era.

536
00:42:01,061 --> 00:42:13,740
The Chicago World's Fair in 1893 represented the zenith of 19th century American technology,

537
00:42:14,880 --> 00:42:19,820
marking the true end of the last century and the dawn of our modern era.

538
00:42:20,600 --> 00:42:24,920
Among those present were three of the greatest visionaries of the time.

539
00:42:26,160 --> 00:42:29,520
George Westinghouse, who invented the air brakes, was there.

540
00:42:29,521 --> 00:42:36,460
His electrical system lighted the fare, astounding the millions of faregoers with its brilliant glow.

541
00:42:36,461 --> 00:42:42,900
He spent his last years devising the alternating current technology that revolutionized America.

542
00:42:42,901 --> 00:42:53,680
George Pullman was there, with his finest cars that epitomized the level of comfort, safety, and prestige that America had attained as a society.

543
00:42:53,681 --> 00:43:02,680
And Jim Hill was there, with his great northern locomotives representing the western expansionism that the fair was celebrating.

544
00:43:02,681 --> 00:43:08,680
Five transcontinental railroads and hundreds of feeder lines now laced the west.

545
00:43:08,681 --> 00:43:18,680
The 200,000 miles of steel had been a colossal feat, a testament to the power of technology and man's ambition to conquer the west.

546
00:43:18,681 --> 00:43:19,483
the West.

547
00:43:19,484 --> 00:43:26,620
As the 19th century drew to a close, the pioneering iron horse was now America's

548
00:43:26,621 --> 00:43:28,123
first big business.

549
00:43:28,124 --> 00:43:33,580
A vast industrial complex manufactured steam locomotives, freight and

550
00:43:33,581 --> 00:43:39,400
passenger cars, and every other need to build and run a railroad.

551
00:43:39,401 --> 00:43:41,460
One major industry that

552
00:43:41,461 --> 00:43:46,422
grew up as a result of the railroad was America's steel industry.

553
00:43:46,423 --> 00:43:48,560
Before steel, railroads used

554
00:43:48,561 --> 00:43:52,320
expensive wrought iron for track building.

555
00:43:52,321 --> 00:43:55,700
But the railroad's demand for a strong, durable alloy

556
00:43:55,701 --> 00:43:58,300
encouraged the growth of the steel industry

557
00:43:58,301 --> 00:44:02,740
in the 1870s and 1880s.

558
00:44:02,741 --> 00:44:05,240
One lasting legacy of the Western railroads

559
00:44:05,241 --> 00:44:07,080
is hard to believe.

560
00:44:07,081 --> 00:44:10,380
Ironically, after three decades of promoting Western

561
00:44:10,381 --> 00:44:13,180
expansionism, the railroads lobbied

562
00:44:13,181 --> 00:44:16,120
for conservation of many natural treasures

563
00:44:16,121 --> 00:44:19,800
that they had made accessible to the public for the first time.

564
00:44:20,800 --> 00:44:23,600
They realized that they had opened a Pandora's box

565
00:44:23,920 --> 00:44:28,400
and that they were permitting the kind of despoilation,

566
00:44:28,401 --> 00:44:31,160
the kind of loss of this wonderful scenery,

567
00:44:31,540 --> 00:44:34,700
and that if they themselves didn't protect it, no one else would.

568
00:44:35,200 --> 00:44:38,200
And if they lost it, the nation lost it, the world lost it,

569
00:44:38,440 --> 00:44:39,380
it was gone forever.

570
00:44:41,080 --> 00:44:44,220
In the years to come, the railroads played a major role

571
00:44:44,221 --> 00:44:47,180
in creating the national park system,

572
00:44:47,181 --> 00:44:50,960
the Grand Canyon, Yosemite, Yellowstone,

573
00:44:50,961 --> 00:44:52,960
and other great natural wonders.

574
00:44:53,920 --> 00:44:56,460
Their grandeur lives on today,

575
00:44:56,461 --> 00:45:00,140
long after the time the steam engine was replaced by diesel.

576
00:45:01,140 --> 00:45:03,580
Many of the tracks which ran across America

577
00:45:03,581 --> 00:45:06,100
are now collecting weeds and rust.

578
00:45:07,140 --> 00:45:09,580
Railroads still haul millions of tons of freight

579
00:45:09,581 --> 00:45:12,140
across country, but for the few people

580
00:45:12,141 --> 00:45:14,540
who now make the journey by rail,

581
00:45:14,541 --> 00:45:17,380
the elegance and mystique have long vanished.

582
00:45:18,980 --> 00:45:21,980
Today Pullman cars are a memory,

583
00:45:21,981 --> 00:45:24,960
eclipsed in the 1930s by the airplane.

584
00:45:27,720 --> 00:45:30,920
But they would enjoy one last moment of glory,

585
00:45:30,921 --> 00:45:33,760
carrying Harry Truman across America

586
00:45:33,761 --> 00:45:36,500
in his 1948 whistle stop campaign.

587
00:45:38,960 --> 00:45:41,100
The legacy of the thousands of men

588
00:45:41,101 --> 00:45:43,860
who worked in the West on the railroad lives on.

589
00:45:45,040 --> 00:45:47,540
Their names may be lost to time,

590
00:45:47,541 --> 00:45:50,940
but their sweat and sacrifice helped build a nation

591
00:45:50,941 --> 00:45:52,120
out of a wilderness.

592
00:45:53,080 --> 00:45:57,760
A land built on freedom, opportunity, dreams,

593
00:45:57,761 --> 00:46:01,860
and thousands of miles of steel rail.

